<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="runners"
      updated="August 7, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"groups-labels", "Groups and labels"},
        {"enrollment-keys", "Enrollment keys"},
        {"pack-credentials", "Giving packs their credentials"},
        {"connectivity", "Online, offline, and stuck runs"},
        {"updating", "Updating"},
        {"toolbox", "The host-side toolbox"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"},
        {"removing", "Removing a runner"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="runners">
        <:dek>
          The
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >quickstart</.link>
          gets one runner online. This page is the rest of its life.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/runner-fleet.webp"
        title="emisar console · Runners"
        caption="The fleet by group — each runner's version, hostname, last heartbeat, and connection status."
        alt="The Runners page: the fleet grouped by runner group — each runner's name, agent version, hostname, last heartbeat, and connection status. One runner carries a signed-only badge because it requires signed dispatch."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="groups-labels">Groups and labels</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every runner declares a <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">group</code>
        (one string — <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">cassandra-prod</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">web</code>) and free-form
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">labels</code>
        (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">role=db</code>, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">region=us-east-1</code>) in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/config.yaml</code>.
        Groups are load-bearing: runbook steps target them, per-member runner scopes follow
        them, and an LLM fan-out call can address every runner in one. Pick group names the way
        you'd name a fleet tier, not a host. The installer defaults the group to the hostname —
        fine for one box, worth changing the moment you have a second.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="enrollment-keys">Enrollment keys</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Two key models.</strong>
            An <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emkey-enroll-…</code>
            key is <strong class="text-zinc-200">single-use</strong>
            by default — spent on its first registration (concurrent attempts with the same key
            can't both win), right for an autoscaler enrolling one host at a time. Mark it
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">reusable</strong>
            and the same key keeps enrolling hosts until it expires or hits its max-uses cap —
            leave the cap blank for unlimited. Right for image bakes and stable fleets.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Shown once, traded for a token.</strong>
            The raw key appears once, at creation. On first connect the runner trades it for its
            own token
            (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">rnrtok-…</code>, stored
            hashed cloud-side, mode-0600 on the host) — the key itself is never presented again
            by that host, whichever model minted it. That token expires after 90 days and the
            runner refreshes it at 60, without anyone touching the machine; the schedule and its
            one prerequisite are in <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/credentials"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >rotate and revoke credentials</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Mint at install time.</strong>
            <a
              href={~p"/app/runners/install"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Runners → Connect a runner</a>
            generates the install one-liner with a
            fresh single-use key baked in. For fleet provisioning (cloud-init, Packer, Ansible),
            mint one reusable key under
            <a
              href={~p"/app/runners/keys"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Runners → Enrollment keys</a>
            and inject it as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Revocation is cloud-side.</strong>
            Revoking a key blocks new registrations; hosts already enrolled keep running on
            their own tokens. Disable a runner to block it reversibly — the host service stays up and
            keeps retrying until you re-enable it. Delete a runner to revoke its token permanently: its
            next connect gets a 401 and the service exits rather than retrying forever.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="pack-credentials">Giving packs their credentials</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Packs that talk to a service — Nomad, Consul, Postgres — read credentials from the
        runner's <em>environment</em>, never from call arguments, so secrets never transit the
        cloud. Two steps on the host:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="config"><span class="text-zinc-500"># 1. /etc/emisar/runner.env (mode 0600) — the values</span>
NOMAD_ADDR=http://127.0.0.1:4646
NOMAD_TOKEN=&lt;acl-token&gt;

<span class="text-zinc-500"># 2. /etc/emisar/config.yaml — allowlist the names</span>
execution:
  inherit_env:
    - NOMAD_ADDR
    - NOMAD_TOKEN</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Only allowlisted names reach an action's process (on top of the <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">PATH</code>/locale baseline) — the
        runner's own environment and its token never leak through. Restart the service after
        editing; what each pack needs is in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar pack info &lt;id&gt;</code>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="connectivity">Online, offline, and stuck runs</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner heartbeats every 30 seconds; the dashboard's online badge reflects it, and
        either side closes a connection that goes quiet. Disconnects are normal — the runner
        reconnects with exponential backoff and re-advertises its catalog, and in-flight actions
        keep executing through the gap with their results delivered on the new connection. If a
        runner stays offline past a two-minute grace, its in-flight runs are marked as errored
        with an explanation — nothing sits "running" forever.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="updating">Updating</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># runner binary — re-run the installer; configs and packs are preserved</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl --proto '=https' --proto-redir '=https' --globoff -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo bash

<span class="text-zinc-500"># packs — update from the registry; the update reloads the runner (no restart, no dropped runs)</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack update linux-core</.docs_code>
      <.docs_verify_download tarball="emisar-<version>-linux-amd64.tar.gz" checksums="SHA256SUMS" />
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Reload (SIGHUP) re-reads packs and re-advertises the catalog on the live connection. A
        changed pack hash lands as <strong class="text-zinc-200">pending</strong> on the Packs
        page until someone trusts it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Those are the two commands. Rolling them across a fleet — canary, verification, batches, and
        the way back — is
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/upgrades"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >upgrade runners and MCP bridges</.link>
        for the binary and
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >roll out and roll back packs</.link>
        for pack bytes. A binary upgrade never moves packs, and a pack update never moves the
        binary.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="toolbox">The host-side toolbox</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> journalctl -u emisar -f          <span class="text-zinc-500"># service logs</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar action list           <span class="text-zinc-500"># what this host advertises</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar pack list             <span class="text-zinc-500"># loaded packs + hashes</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar events tail -f        <span class="text-zinc-500"># the local JSONL journal, live</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar audit verify --all    <span class="text-zinc-500"># verify the journal's hash chain</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        (The CLI defaults to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">--config /etc/emisar/config.yaml</code>,
        so these work as-is on an installed host.) The runner itself runs as the unprivileged
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar</code>
        user, so a pack action that needs root — restarting a system service, editing system config —
        runs only if you grant that user the right: a scoped sudoers rule (e.g. <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx</code>,
        with the action calling <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo</code>) or an
        equivalent polkit rule. For that plus the other host defense-in-depth controls — the local
        admission allowlist, systemd hardening drop-ins — see the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security model</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The four host-side failures you'll actually meet. To start from a symptom anywhere else —
        a run that won't dispatch, an LLM client that can't connect, sign-in or directory sync, a
        poller that stopped — use <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >troubleshooting</.link>, which routes each one to its owner page.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Never shows up:</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">journalctl -u emisar -f</code>.
            A 401 on a first connect means the enrollment key was spent, expired, out of uses, or
            revoked — mint a fresh one. A connect timeout means outbound 443 is blocked: the runner
            needs <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar.dev</code>, the
            installer also needs
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">github.com</code>
            and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">api.github.com</code>, and
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar pack install</code>
            needs the registry — all outbound, no inbound rule.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Service is "failed":</strong> after five crashes in
            five minutes systemd stops retrying (deliberate — a revoked key shouldn't hammer the
            cloud). Fix the cause, then <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">systemctl reset-failed emisar && systemctl start emisar</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Action won't dispatch:</strong>
            check
            <a
              href={~p"/app/packs"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Packs</a>
            first — a pending (untrusted) or drifted pack hash blocks dispatch by design. Then
            check the member's or key's runner and pack scope.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pack refuses to load:</strong>
            the journal names the
            exact file and rule — duplicate action IDs, a script escaping the pack root, and
            symlinks (without <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">allow_symlinks</code>)
            all fail closed.
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
              emisar pack validate ./pack
            </code>
            reproduces it locally.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="removing">Removing a runner</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell"><span class="text-zinc-500"># remove binary + service + cached token</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo bash install.sh --uninstall
<span class="text-zinc-500"># …or purge everything, including the local journal</span>
<span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo bash install.sh --uninstall --purge</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Then delete the runner from <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>. Its run history and audit events stay.
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">--purge</code>
        deletes the local
        journal, configuration, and
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">runner.env</code>
        secrets too, so export the journal first if you keep host-side forensics.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        That is the routine retirement. If you are removing the host because you no longer trust it,
        the order changes — evidence first, then containment — and deleting the runner is terminal
        where disabling is not. Follow
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-incidents" <> "#runner-host"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >security incidents</.link>
        instead.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="changing-enrollment-key">Changing the enrollment key</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Supply a different
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY</code>
        when reinstalling. The runner detects the changed key, re-registers its configured
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">runner.id</code>
        or current hostname, and replaces the cached token on its next connection.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A replacement host with a new hostname enrolls as a new runner automatically. No
        disk-backed identity file or identity-reset step is involved.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
